# Academious scheduled work.
#
#   install:  crontab /srv/academious/deploy/crontab
#   inspect:  crontab -l
#   remove:   crontab -r
#
# Prerequisites, once:
#   mkdir -p /var/log/academious
#
# Times are in the host timezone, which deployment.md sets to UTC. The nightly
# entries sit at 02:30 and 03:45 UTC, inside NCBI's requested off-peak window
# (21:00-05:00 US Eastern = 01:00-09:00 UTC in summer, 02:00-10:00 in winter)
# under both US daylight and standard time. Moving them means re-checking that.
#
# Every entry is wrapped in `flock -n`, which skips the run rather than queuing
# it when the previous one is still going. Without it a cron tick that outlives
# its interval stacks containers until the box runs out of memory - and the
# embed job routinely runs for hours, so that is a certainty rather than a risk.
#
# `docker compose` needs no -f flags because COMPOSE_FILE is set in .env; see
# "Pinning the compose files" in deployment.md.

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
MAILTO=""

# Harvest every source, hourly at ten past.
# Incremental: each source resumes from its stored cursor, so a skipped or
# failed run costs freshness, never data.
10 * * * * cd /srv/academious && flock -n /var/lock/academious-harvest.lock docker compose run --rm worker python -m academious.workers harvest --source all >> /var/log/academious/harvest.log 2>&1

# Embed whatever the harvest added, every 30 minutes.
#
# This entry is the one whose absence is invisible. A harvest that adds papers
# nothing embeds still succeeds, the feed still shows the new papers, and only
# semantic search quietly answers from a shrinking share of the corpus - which
# is exactly what happened on 2026-09-01, when one OpenAlex run added 42,609
# papers and search coverage fell from 30% to 13% with nothing reporting a
# failure.
#
# Each invocation drains up to MAX_PENDING_SCAN (10,000) papers and exits, at
# roughly 1 paper/second. The frequent schedule plus flock is what turns that
# into a steady drain: ticks landing while a run is still going are skipped, so
# a large backlog is worked through by successive runs rather than by one long
# job that must not be interrupted.
*/30 * * * * cd /srv/academious && flock -n /var/lock/academious-embed.lock docker compose run --rm worker python -m academious.workers embed >> /var/log/academious/embed.log 2>&1

# Preprint -> published DOI map, nightly. bioRxiv publishes the only
# authoritative version of this, and it is the hardest part of deduplication.
30 2 * * * cd /srv/academious && flock -n /var/lock/academious-links.lock docker compose run --rm worker python -m academious.workers link-publications >> /var/log/academious/links.log 2>&1

# Retraction Watch, nightly. ~66 MB download, so it does not belong on an
# hourly schedule. Retracted papers are excluded from discovery by default, so
# a stale run means recommending work that has been withdrawn.
45 3 * * * cd /srv/academious && flock -n /var/lock/academious-retractions.lock docker compose run --rm worker python -m academious.workers retractions >> /var/log/academious/retractions.log 2>&1
